I cannot reproduce the outage. End-to-end reachability is healthy: pc1 (195.11.14.2) pings pc2 (200.1.1.2) successfully with 0% loss and sub-millisecond RTT. The BGP subsystem between our routers is up and exchanging the expected prefixes. On router1, the route to 200.1.1.0/24 is learned via BGP from 193.10.11.2 (router2) and installed as best. On router2, 195.11.14.0/24 is learned via BGP from 193.10.11.1 (router1). Both routers’ “show ip bgp summary” report one neighbor, one prefix received, and the sessions are up.

Mechanism and localization: If the partner’s routes had been withdrawn, router1 would lack a path to 200.1.1.0/24 and pc1->200.1.1.2 would fail. Currently, router1 shows “B>* 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2” and router2 shows “B>* 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1,” confirming proper BGP propagation over the inter-router link 193.10.11.0/24 (router1:eth0 <-> router2:eth0).

Notably, the BGP Up/Down timers on both sides are ~1.3 minutes, indicating the session was recently reset; this could explain a brief earlier outage when the ticket was filed. Root cause: a transient BGP session reset between router1 and router2 briefly withdrew the partner routes but has since recovered. Corrective fix: none needed now; monitor for recurrence, enable BGP logging/BFD or graceful restart to minimize impact of future resets, and review recent changes/maintenance that may have caused the flap.
